# Local Notes — working development version

## Current source epoch: browser draft recovery

The current source includes bounded browser recovery copies for unsaved note and to-do drafts. The exact experimental source `f0ca90d2cfafcc10f102c88b7dce3ef3c895d269` passed 128 finite checks and 12 Chromium scenarios on verified hosted Windows. The twelve scenarios comprise four user workflows and eight native IndexedDB cases. The [qualified checkpoint](https://github.com/Ginderlax/rea-research-lab/blob/4107879691f5480b0ac9a5ea29f360ee336504a3/products/rea-studio/results/notes-draft-recovery-20261018/CHECKPOINT.md) preserves those exact receipts and the original failed attempt. Installation requires its own source binding, retained earlier browser assertions and complete-suite evidence; earlier results are never rebound to this source.

Copies remain plaintext in this browser, separated by origin and a stable workspace UUID, with at most four records of 2 MiB each and 8 MiB total. They preserve raw title, body, tags, notebook selection and draft type, without attachment binaries. Recovery requires explicit acknowledgement and opens a separate unsaved note or to-do. Only an explicit Save persists it. An earlier Save may already have committed, so the interface warns users to check saved notes before deliberately creating a separate copy. Recovery never automatically replays an uncertain request or overwrites the original saved note. A missing notebook produces a destination warning.

Unavailable or corrupt browser storage leaves the active editor and ordinary Save available. Clearing browser data or stopping the browser before a write commits can still lose text. These copies are excluded from saved-note exports and do not provide encrypted exported backups, original Joplin autosave/crash lifecycle parity, complete browser compatibility or accessibility acceptance. The earlier proposal sections below retain their historical scope; current acceptance comes only from receipts bound to the corresponding source epoch.

The prospective inline-confirmation candidate replaces native browser dialogs for notebook Trash and Restore with visible Confirm/Cancel controls. It names the selected notebook and recursive scope, initially focuses Cancel, supports Escape cancellation and a two-button Tab cycle, and makes the background inert while pending. After Confirm, both choices are disabled and focus stays on the pending dialog until the request settles. Cancel and closing Notes before Confirm submit no notebook operation. Closing after a committed request suppresses its old view publication; it does not undo the commit. Dirty drafts must still be saved first. Backend notebook recursion, revision and confirmation contracts are unchanged. Other actions retain their existing native dialogs. DOM model tests verify finite keyboard/focus behavior; actual browser and assistive-technology acceptance require separate review.

An independently written local Markdown notebook with bounded Joplin JEX interoperability and source-assisted notebook organization. It is **not a complete Joplin replacement**. The application does not import the earlier Joplin reconstruction or run the original application as a wrapper.

## Implemented

- Persisted notes with title, body, notebook, tags, dates, optimistic revisions, last 20 saved versions, restoration and recoverable trash.
- AND substring search across title/body/tag/notebook text, plus tag and notebook filters. This differs from Joplin full-text term matching and search grammar.
- Safe DOM-rendered Markdown subset, Markdown imports/exports and legacy JSON note-copy interchange.
- **JEX imports and exports** using real uncompressed TAR archives and raw record types: note (1), folder (2), resource (4), tag (5) and note-tag relation (6).
- Original Joplin IDs, note/resource links, folder parent IDs and nested paths, tag IDs and relationships are retained. Supported raw metadata—including timestamps, geolocation, author, source URL, to-do fields, notebook icons and application data—is preserved rather than replaced with import-time values. Metadata without an implemented UI stays available for round-trip export; this does not implement the corresponding original feature.
- Binary file attachment upload, list and download. Upload inserts a Joplin `:/resource-id` link into the saved note. Download and JEX export verify the stored byte count and SHA-256. Files are stored under the dedicated confined Notes workspace using generated IDs and content hashes, never supplied filenames.
- Exact `:/32-hex-id` Markdown links open a local note or download a resource referenced by the saved source note. Opening another note asks before discarding a dirty draft; self-links retain the draft. Missing or unsupported targets report an error. Resource links use the same integrity-checked download path. This subset does not implement heading anchors, image previews or Joplin external URL handlers.
- Imported IDs are preserved. Any collision rejects the whole archive instead of overwriting or remapping items. Unknown record types/fields, encryption, unsupported HTML notes, missing binaries, unresolved note/resource links, broken or duplicate tag relationships and cyclic notebook parents reject before saving.
- Notes and related metadata commit through one atomic state record. Binary files are prepared first and cleaned up after a failed metadata commit. Existing legacy note files migrate on the first mutation and remain preserved; the new state is authoritative afterward.

Choose **Export Joplin JEX** for the supported current notes, notebook/tag metadata and attachments. The **Show trash** checkbox also controls whether trashed notes are exported. References to omitted notes cause export to fail; include trash when a retained note links to a trashed note. All retained notebook/tag/resource records are included, including empty notebooks and presently unreferenced resource binaries. JEX does not contain this app's saved-version history, nor does it back up the application's entire local state.

Notebook hierarchy imported from JEX is preserved and shown as `Parent / Child`. The local organization panel creates, renames and reparents notebooks, and moves a saved note using stable notebook IDs. Exact-ID filters distinguish notebooks with identical titles. Renaming or moving a parent updates descendant display paths without changing native IDs or note content. Folder and note moves preserve original user timestamps, while recording the system update timestamp. Organization and note revisions reject stale requests; a dirty browser draft must be saved before organization changes. The legacy API notebook text field still creates a flat notebook with that title. Existing folders and unknown supported metadata remain retained. This prospective stage adds confirmed notebook trash and restore for local unshared valid trees. A cascade preserves native IDs, tag/resource links and binary bytes and commits through one state record. Notebook restore also restores independently trashed notes in restored folders. A trashed parent remains in trash; restoring its child detaches that child to top level. Folder trash updates user and system times; note trash and all restoration preserve user times. Trashed notebooks are selectable for restoration, while rename/move still refuse them. Shared organization, the reserved conflict notebook, permanent deletion, empty trash, purge, optional restore targets and sharing/synchronization remain outside this subset. Tag edits preserve existing identities and relation metadata when possible; ambiguous identities fail rather than merge silently.

## Supported archive bounds

The archive limit is 8 MiB, each binary resource 4 MiB, at most 1,000 notes, 2,000 raw records and 3,000 TAR entries, and notebook depth 64. Notes support 200,000 UTF-16 characters, titles 300, displayed notebook paths 200, and at most 50 tags of 100 characters each. State storage is subject to the host's 16 MiB record limit, including saved histories; a failed commit keeps the previous state.

New or changed note titles, notebook labels and tags must fit on one line; API saves and JSON imports reject CR/LF before changing any saved state. Existing saved labels are preserved verbatim, including when editing another field. A legacy multiline label causes JEX export to fail explicitly until the label is edited; JSON export retains the original note text and labels, but still excludes attachment bytes. Incoming raw JEX record titles must also fit on one line.

TAR accepts regular USTAR-compatible files, optional `resources/` directory entries, complete zero-padded blocks and a valid end marker. It rejects path traversal, absolute paths, duplicate file paths, links, PAX/GNU extensions, compression, special entries, malformed checksums and trailing nonzero data. Parsed header names and numeric fields require exact ASCII bytes; high-bit bytes are rejected rather than aliased to ASCII. Resource extension names are limited to 20 ASCII letters/digits/underscore/hyphen. When an original resource lacks an explicit extension, a bounded MIME-to-extension table covers common formats; other MIME fallbacks reject. Missing referenced notebooks and linked notes reject instead of creating replacement locations or broken identities. These strict limits mean **not every legitimate JEX archive is supported**.

## Run and verify

From `products/rea-studio`, run `npm start` and select **Notes**. From the repository root:

```sh
node --test --test-concurrency=1 products/rea-studio/apps/notes/app.test.mjs products/rea-studio/apps/notes/app.integration.test.mjs products/rea-studio/apps/notes/jex.test.mjs products/rea-studio/apps/notes/notebooks.test.mjs products/rea-studio/apps/notes/notebooks.integration.test.mjs
```

The local test suite requires Node 24+ and a system `tar` command for independent archive verification; the application itself has no package dependencies or TAR subprocess. Tests cover original-format import/export, exact metadata and resource bytes, native TAR parsing of app exports, stale/concurrent edits, collision rejection, malicious headers, broken relationships, storage rollback, binary tampering, legacy migration and HTTP cold restarts with attachments. Markdown and legacy core tests remain included.

The checked-in synthetic fixture was generated by executing the exact pinned official `BaseItem.serialize_format`, `unserialize_format`, `serialize` and `unserialize` methods, then packing with native TAR. Its [provenance](fixtures/pinned-joplin.json) fingerprints the official source bytes, archive and raw records. Unmodified app exports match every original serialized raw record hash byte for byte when independently extracted. The separate [original-parser receipt](fixtures/oracle-verification.json) records **22 passing checks**, including app-generated notes, edited metadata, new notebook/tag relationships and resource bytes. Verification downloads only the pinned source for these four methods; it uses a narrow harness with pinned field definitions and minimal database/date stubs. It does **not** execute the complete Joplin application, database, importer or user interface.

To regenerate the synthetic fixture deliberately:

```sh
node products/rea-studio/apps/notes/interop-fixture-tool.mjs generate
```

The reference source is pinned to the studied renderer registry source head `c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5`. Renderer `3.7.1` is not equated with desktop `3.7.1`; complete-product release correspondence remains unresolved. Contracts came from the pinned [JEX/RAW documentation](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/readme/apps/import_export.md), [raw exporter](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/packages/lib/services/interop/InteropService_Exporter_Raw.ts), [raw importer](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/packages/lib/services/interop/InteropService_Importer_Raw.ts), [item serialization](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/packages/lib/models/BaseItem.ts), [database entity schema](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/packages/lib/services/database/types.ts) and [resource filename rules](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5/packages/lib/models/utils/resourceUtils.ts). Original source is used only by isolated verification tooling, without redistribution of the original implementation in the application.

The new notebook study separately binds to released **Joplin Desktop 3.6.14**, tag `v3.6.14`, commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`; official tag, release metadata and Desktop package version agree. Retained official [Folder helpers](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d/packages/lib/models/Folder.ts), [Note move helper](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d/packages/lib/models/Note.ts), [subnotebook documentation](https://github.com/laurent22/joplin/blob/7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d/readme/apps/subnotebooks.md) and licenses authenticate the selected behavior. The test-only oracle executes exact helper slices using Node's TypeScript stripping, an in-memory load/save adapter, a fixed clock and English messages. Its finite regular, unshared fixtures provide **26 exact candidate comparisons**: 20 nesting decisions, four ancestor paths and two exported move effects. Two additional original move executions assert the original contract without counting as exact candidate comparisons. The complete original application, database, importer and GUI are not executed. This does not rebind the older 22-check serialization receipt to the new release.

Original retained source and test-only slices carry the original AGPL-3.0-or-later notices; see the [preserved study notice](results/notebooks-followup/stage/NOTICE.md.snapshot), [source provenance](results/notebooks-followup/stage/source-provenance.json.snapshot) and [license/reserved-ID provenance](results/notebooks-followup/stage/license-and-special-id-provenance.json.snapshot) before redistributing the study. Candidate organization code is source-assisted, newly written code; no MIT or clean-room licensing claim is made. These bounds intentionally differ from original unbounded library helpers: 2,000 records, depth 64 and a 200-character display path. Duplicate sibling titles remain distinct by ID. Move comparisons cover regular unshared folders and notes only; original shared-folder permissions, database races and GUI drag/drop are open acceptance gaps.

## Remaining full-product gaps

Still missing: full CommonMark/Joplin Markdown compatibility, rich-text editing, inline image rendering, heading anchors and external Joplin URL handlers, complete attachment preview/download policies and automatic resource cleanup, encryption/key management, synchronization and conflicts, native/mobile/terminal/web clients, web clipper, plugin APIs, to-do alarms and editing controls, geolocation UI, complete notebook lifecycle and original drag/drop behavior, sharing/publishing, complete JEX/RAW and other format interoperability, OCR, advanced search/ranking, autosave/crash recovery, accessibility parity, profiles and original client/service APIs. Strict archive rejection and bounded fixture checks are not evidence of full import/export parity or superiority.

Notes and binaries remain plaintext locally. Unsaved drafts can have bounded plaintext browser recovery copies; restoring one requires explicit separate-copy acknowledgement and another Save. A browser stop before the write commits or clearing browser data can still lose the draft. JSON import creates fresh note IDs/timestamps and rejects Joplin metadata or internal links because that format cannot preserve related binaries/identities. JSON and standalone Markdown exports exclude resource binaries; the UI identifies JSON as incomplete backup material. A crash between binary preparation and metadata commit may leave an unreferenced binary file; it does not expose a partially imported note state. No automatic permanent purge is provided. All undocumented or untested behavior remains an open acceptance gap.

The retained [original scope](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/README.md.snapshot), [source contracts](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/SOURCE-CONTRACTS.md.snapshot) and [notice](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/NOTICE.md.snapshot) bind the additional Desktop 3.6.14 study. Original code remains test-only; the finite runtime is source-assisted and has no full notebook parity claim. The new exact helper comparisons use memory adapters and a fixed clock, not the original application/database/GUI.


The current notebook trash/restore correction captures editor generation, input revision and all four draft values for Save, guards both asynchronous refresh reads, and captures the two new lifecycle commands as four primitives before queueing. These guards preserve later drafts and newer notebook/search results in finite held-response cases; they do not cancel committed requests or establish universal lifecycle/input-ownership guarantees. The frozen correction has 55 author cases; the separate independent review has 18 passing local cases (seven original reviewer bodies, seven adjacent controls, four new controls). Its new original helper execution is one authenticated nine-slice context/top-level restore with eleven item projections and 55 scalar assertions. Complete original application/database/GUI executions are zero. Historical 22 serialization, 26 notebook organization comparisons, original trash campaign and new correction/review counts remain separate.

The independent Chrome attempt reached a genuine saved note and held a completed actual HTTP Save, but browser dialog/focus control stalled at the native JavaScript trash confirmation. No notebook-trash request reached the server and actual trash/restore browser acceptance is incomplete in that receipt. Its owned host and temporary data were cleaned; owned Chrome tab closure was unconfirmed. Source-only helper/oracle routes remain private. Retained [correction notice](results/notebook-trash-followup/publication-fix/NOTICE.md.snapshot) and [independent review](results/notebook-trash-followup/publication-review/REVIEW.md.snapshot) explain these finite limits. Full product parity, security parity and superiority remain unestablished.

## Prospective to-do editing stage — unexecuted

This separate source-only candidate adds saved-note conversion between note and to-do, explicit complete/reopen controls, due-date metadata editing/clearing with millisecond precision, and note/open/completed to-do list filters. Save the note draft first. Due dates do not schedule alarms. Existing updated-time sorting remains unchanged. This candidate is not installed or runtime-verified.

To-do changes require the saved note revision and use one atomic state commit. Unchanged desired states avoid additional writes/revisions. Imported original metadata stays unchanged until edited; edited to-do states export through JEX while retaining IDs, resources, tags and unrelated metadata. JSON copies preserve validated to-do state while still excluding attachment bytes. New history snapshots retain to-do state; older snapshots without it cannot restore unknown historical values. Shared/locked/trashed-note editing is refused by this finite local policy.

The authored tests include twenty-three groups, one containing twenty planned comparisons against two exact authenticated Joplin Desktop 3.6.14 helpers. None has executed. They do not establish original application/database/GUI parity. New to-do creation, default/configurable original sorting, full search, alarms, synchronization, accessibility, genuine browser acceptance and all other full-product gaps remain open.
The corrected source stage uses the existing public core.mjs browser-module route and authors exact HTTP delivery checks for the client's dependencies. Original helper comparisons observe candidate results even for unchanged type conversions. Pending mutation errors and final status messages respect newer editor/draft ownership; two additional held-response cases cover those boundaries. These are source corrections awaiting independent review and hosted execution.


## New To-do: separate future source proposal

This separate, unexecuted candidate adds a **New to-do** draft next to New note. Choosing it stores nothing. Give the draft a title, choose a notebook/tags and write its body, then choose Save note. A successful first save writes the note and its open to-do state together at revision1, with no due date, completion date or prior saved versions. Saved-note controls can then set due metadata, complete/reopen or convert it. New note resets the draft type. Cancelling a discard keeps the previous draft. Attachments still require a saved note. Existing filters and updated-time sorting remain unchanged.

A pending save blocks duplicate saves while typing and starting another draft remain possible. A late success or failure cannot replace a newer editor or a disposed view. A newer body typed while a successful response is held stays unsaved at the returned revision and needs another explicit Save. A persistence failure retains the draft and supports explicit retry. These are authored candidate policies, pending independent review and actual hosted execution.

The source contract uses Joplin Desktop3.6.14's pinned to-do documentation, Note.new/newTodo helpers and desktop newTodo/newNote commands. The original desktop command creates a provisional saved item; this candidate instead keeps an unsaved local draft until explicit Save. Planned exact helper-field and command-argument comparisons are narrow adapters. They do not execute Joplin's full database, provisional lifecycle, command framework, GUI, alarms or synchronization, and do not prove parity. This future epoch leaves the earlier92/8 and installed-source evidence unchanged.


## R5: correction after the first hosted New To-do experiment

The earlier R4 experimental sources ran in hosted study38926216286 and failed:107 of111 finite groups passed and7 of8 Chromium cases passed. Three unchanged notebook ownership regressions showed that the new general Save-busy gate blocked an existing notebook confirmation workflow. The R5 proposal restores that admission while each Save/organization cleanup preserves the other busy owner, deleted-note disabling and duplicate-save/to-do mutation guards. All17 earlier Notes test files remain unchanged. This corrected R5 source proposal is unexecuted; the failed R4 receipt remains historical evidence.

Existing unchanged ensureOrganization materializes metadataEdited:false on a read copy of a first-save note. Invalid400/GET/restart/list operations must preserve every original persisted byte. A later valid write can persist that existing normalization. Full raw-note invariance across unrelated valid New To-do creation is tested only after a real ordinary second Save establishes a canonical revision2/history1/metadataEdited:true baseline; no field is dropped. The integration test also compares the complete normalized GET result before and after every invalid request and restart. These precise comparison boundaries do not claim that every first-save raw note byte remains unchanged across all later valid writes.


## Separate future proposal: explicit search filters (unexecuted)

The current default Text search remains AND substring search over title, body, tag and notebook text. A separate Filters choice adds `title:`, `body:`, `tag:`, `notebook:`, `type:note`/`type:todo` and `iscompleted:0`/`iscompleted:1`. Every ordinary clause must match. Prefix text, title, body, tag or notebook clauses with `-` to exclude their matches. The existing tag/notebook/to-do/trash selectors continue to restrict the results independently. Search never saves, restores or changes a note or draft.

This source-assisted grammar study is pinned to Joplin Desktop 3.6.14, commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`. The parser preserves the selected original syntax rules: quoted title/body values split into separate terms; unmatched quotes close at the end; type/completion values are case-sensitive and those two filters cannot be negated. Unknown filter names remain text. `tag:` and `notebook:` use SQLite-style label patterns: `*` and `%` match any number of characters, `_` matches one Unicode code point, ASCII letter matching ignores case, and non-ASCII case remains distinct. Notebook patterns match each notebook's own title, include its descendants, and combine positive notebook patterns with OR; negative patterns exclude their descendant scopes. Duplicate notebook titles remain distinct by ID and both matching trees are searched.

This mode uses literal case-insensitive substrings for text/title/body. Free text searches title/body; it does not implicitly search tags or notebook names. A quoted free-text clause is a contiguous substring; this differs from original FTS token phrases. Title/body clauses split on the pinned parser's whitespace, hyphen and underscore separators. Queries are limited to 500 UTF-16 characters and 64 parsed terms. Parsed nonempty `any:`, date, resource, source-URL, ID and location clauses report an explicit unsupported-mode error, as do text/title/body wildcards. As in the pinned parser, a field with no value, such as `any:`, contributes no term. No parsed terms means no query restriction. An explicitly empty substring such as `title:""` matches every title; this is a local policy, not evidence of original FTS behavior. The updated-time ordering remains unchanged. SQLite FTS tokenization, Unicode normalization and diacritic removal, HTML-entity decoding, BM25, multilingual modes, OCR, full grammar, original application/database/GUI and accessibility parity remain open.

The work-only source includes authored parser comparisons against the exact standalone original parser and metadata-only SQL comparisons against exact original query-builder bodies using a synthetic SQLite database and a denied unused date adapter. These comparisons, product tests and browser checks have not executed. No runtime acceptance, search parity or superiority is claimed. This is a separate future epoch and does not rebind Notes41 draft-recovery or any earlier receipts.
